ArchiveAscii: pad the top-level `objects` count to 9 characters, as ZenGin does.

The ASCII header's object count is a fixed-width field. ZenGin writes it 9
characters wide; `WriteArchiveAscii::write_header` terminated its scratch
buffer at index 11 and wrote an 11-wide one, so every ASCII archive ZenKit
produces differs from an engine-written one in the header even when every
object below it is byte-identical.

Measured against retail `OldCamp.zen` (docs/engine-acceptance-2026-08-25.md
§10.2, defect A4): `objects 1835     ` in the original against
`objects 1835       ` in the re-save.

This is the same defect patch 0013 fixed for `zCArchiverBinary`, which padded
to 10; the two writers had picked two different wrong widths.

--- a/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
@@ -455,9 +455,12 @@
 		this->_m_write->write_line(username);
 		this->_m_write->write_line("END");
 
+		// ZenGin pads the object count to 9 characters, not 11 (`objects 1835 `
+		// plus five spaces in retail OldCamp.zen). The field is fixed-width in
+		// the file, so a wider one is a byte difference on every ASCII archive.
 		memset(date_buffer, ' ', 20);
-		date_buffer[11] = '\0';
-		std::to_chars(date_buffer, date_buffer + 10, _m_index);
+		date_buffer[9] = '\0';
+		std::to_chars(date_buffer, date_buffer + 9, _m_index);
 
 		this->_m_write->write_string("objects ");
 		this->_m_write->write_line(date_buffer);
